    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"12e2b2ca\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# Retrieving Climate Technology News From GDELT: A Documented, Filtered Pipeline\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Author:** Cali Pfleger, Bren School of Environmental Science and Management, University of California, Santa Barbara\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Version:** 1.0 (July 2026)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Purpose:** Retrieve English-language, US-source news headlines about climate intervention technologies from GDELT, filtering at the query so only relevant articles are downloaded.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### What this notebook does, in one paragraph\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This notebook collects news article metadata (headline, link, publication date, source outlet, language, and source country) for a set of climate-technology search terms, using the free GDELT DOC 2.0 API. It filters for English-language and US-based sources *at the query level*, meaning GDELT only returns articles that already match those conditions, rather than downloading everything and discarding the rest. It saves three files: the raw pull, a cleaned and deduplicated version, and (optionally) a version with the full article body text scraped from each link, ready for sentiment scoring in a later step.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Who this notebook is for\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"It is written to be followed by someone new to Python or to GDELT. Every code cell is preceded by an explanation of what it does and why. Technical terms are defined the first time they appear. You can run the cells top to bottom without prior setup beyond installing a few packages (Section 1).\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"### Conventions\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Output is plain ASCII text. The pipeline is deliberately transparent (no hidden dependencies) so it can be cited and audited in a methods chapter.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"2481a3a1\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"## Table of Contents\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"1. Setup and installation\\n\",\n",
    "    \"2. Background: what GDELT and the DOC API are\\n\",\n",
    "    \"3. Configuration: keywords, filters, and dates\\n\",\n",
    "    \"4. Building a filtered query\\n\",\n",
    "    \"5. The retrieval function\\n\",\n",
    "    \"6. Working around the record limit (time chunking)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"7. Running the retrieval (looping over terms) and saving the raw CSV\\n\",\n",
    "    \"8. Reconciliation and quality checks\\n\",\n",
    "    \"9. Cleaning, deduplication, and saving the cleaned CSV\\n\",\n",
    "    \"10. Scraping the article body text and saving the full corpus\\n\",\n",
    "    \"11. Optional: sentiment and volume over time (triangulation)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"12. Limitations\\n\",\n",
    "    \"13. References and resources\\n\",\n",
    "    \"14. Export this notebook as a shareable HTML report\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"4ca9873b\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"## 1. Setup and Installation\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A **package** is a reusable bundle of Python code. This notebook uses three:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **requests** - sends requests over the internet to the GDELT server and receives the reply.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **pandas** - stores the results in a table (called a *DataFrame*) and makes them easy to clean and save.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- The **standard library** modules (`time`, `datetime`, `json`) come with Python and need no installation.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"If you have never installed these, remove the `#` from the first line below and run the cell once. The `#` marks a *comment*, which Python ignores.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 1,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"ffcead9f\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"name\": \"stdout\",\n",
    "     \"output_type\": \"stream\",\n",
    "     \"text\": [\n",
    "      \"Packages loaded. Ready to retrieve.\\n\"\n",
    "     ]\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"pip install requests pandas# <- run once if these are not installed\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import time                               # to pause politely between requests\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import json                               # to read the server's JSON reply\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import datetime as dt                     # to work with dates\\n\",\n",
    "    \"from urllib.parse import urlencode        # to build a safe web address\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import requests                           # to talk to the GDELT server\\n\",\n",
    "    \"import pandas as pd                       # to hold results in a table\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# A short label identifying us to the server (courtesy, and avoids some blocks)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"USER_AGENT = {\\\"User-Agent\\\": \\\"Mozilla/5.0 (academic research; climate-tech-sentiment)\\\"}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(\\\"Packages loaded. Ready to retrieve.\\\")\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"65e68c77\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"## 2. Background: What GDELT and the DOC API Are\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**GDELT** (the Global Database of Events, Language, and Tone) continuously monitors online news worldwide and makes the results freely available (Leetaru and Schrodt, 2013). We use one part of it: the **DOC 2.0 API**.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"An **API** (Application Programming Interface) is a way for a program to ask a server for data and get a structured reply. The GDELT DOC API takes a search query and returns a list of matching news articles as **JSON** (a simple, machine-readable text format). No account, password, or payment is needed.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"For each article, the DOC API returns:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Field we keep | Meaning |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"|---------------|---------|\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `title` | the article headline |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `url` | the web link to the article |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `domain` | the outlet's website (for example, forbes.com) |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `seendate` | when GDELT first saw the article |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `language` | the article's language |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| `sourcecountry` | the country where the outlet is based |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Two limits to know.** The DOC API returns at most **250 articles per request**, and it ranks results by relevance rather than returning everything. We handle the 250 limit by splitting the search into shorter time windows (Section 6). The API covers roughly 2017 to the present reliably; for older coverage a different GDELT method (bulk files) is needed.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"b73688e6\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"## 3. Configuration: Keywords, Filters, and Dates\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"This is the only section you normally edit. Everything below reads from these settings.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Keywords** are grouped by technology family (`domain`): SRM (solar radiation management), GGR (greenhouse gas removal), and NbS (nature-based solutions). Each phrase is searched separately so you can see coverage per technology. Extend this dictionary to match your full codebook.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Filters** are applied inside the query so GDELT only returns matching articles:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- `FILTER_ENGLISH = True` adds `sourcelang:eng` (English-language sources only).\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- `FILTER_US_SOURCE = True` adds `sourcecountry:US` (US-based outlets only).\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Set either to `False` to widen the search. Note that `sourcecountry:US` filters by where the *outlet* is based, not what the article is about, and it excludes outlets whose country GDELT could not determine - so it is a deliberate narrowing, not a neutral technicality.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 3,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"cc8215cf\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"name\": \"stdout\",\n",
    "     \"output_type\": \"stream\",\n",
    "     \"text\": [\n",
    "      \"Configured 2 keywords across 1 families.\\n\",\n",
    "      \"English-only: True   US-source-only: True\\n\",\n",
    "      \"Date range: 2015-01-01 to 2025-12-30\\n\",\n",
    "      \"Live display: True   Code link: local path\\n\"\n",
    "     ]\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# --- Search terms, grouped by technology family ---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"TECH_DICT = {\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\\"OAE\\\": [\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        \\\"ocean alkalinity enhancement\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"ocean alkalinity enhancement\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"alkalinity enhancement\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"ocean alkalinity\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #ocean alkalinization\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"marine alkalinity\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        \\\"OAE\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"marine carbon removal\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"ocean-based carbon removal\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"ocean-based climate solutions\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"ocean carbon removal\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"marine carbon dioxide removal\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        #\\\"marine CDR\\\",\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    \\n\",\n",
    "    \"    ],\\n\",\n",
    "    \"}\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- Query-level filters (applied BEFORE download) ---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FILTER_ENGLISH   = True     # sourcelang:eng\\n\",\n",
    "    \"FILTER_US_SOURCE = True     # sourcecountry:US\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- Date range to search (UTC). The DOC API is reliable from ~2017 onward. ---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"START_DATE = dt.datetime(2015, 1, 1)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"END_DATE   = dt.datetime(2025, 12, 30)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- Politeness setting: seconds to wait between requests ---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"REQUEST_DELAY = 1.5\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- Where your code lives (used for the link in the HTML report) ---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Two options. Set USE_GITHUB_LINK = True once your GitHub repo exists.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"CODE_PATH_LOCAL = \\\"/Users/calipfleger/Documents/Claude/Projects/Sentiment Analysis\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"CODE_URL_GITHUB = \\\"https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO\\\"   # <- update this\\n\",\n",
    "    \"USE_GITHUB_LINK = False    # False = show the local path; True = show the GitHub link\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- Live display: refresh tables and a chart in place while the pull runs ---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"LIVE_DISPLAY = True        # set False for plain text-only progress\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Flatten the dictionary into (family, keyword) pairs for easy looping\\n\",\n",
    "    \"KEYWORD_PAIRS = [(fam, kw) for fam, kws in TECH_DICT.items() for kw in kws]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Configured {len(KEYWORD_PAIRS)} keywords across {len(TECH_DICT)} families.\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"English-only: {FILTER_ENGLISH}   US-source-only: {FILTER_US_SOURCE}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Date range: {START_DATE.date()} to {END_DATE.date()}\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f\\\"Live display: {LIVE_DISPLAY}   Code link: \\\"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"      f\\\"{'GitHub' if USE_GITHUB_LINK else 'local path'}\\\")\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"63173919\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Link to your code\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The cell below shows a link to your project code in the report. It offers two options set in the configuration above: a local path (works on your own computer) or a GitHub URL (works for anyone, once your repository is set up). Flip `USE_GITHUB_LINK` to `True` when your repo is live.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 4,\n",
    "   \"id\": \"641073c5\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"data\": {\n",
    "      \"text/html\": [\n",
    "       \"<p><b>Source code (local):</b> <code>/Users/calipfleger/Documents/Claude/Projects/Sentiment Analysis</code><br><i>Local paths are not clickable outside your computer; switch to the GitHub link for a shareable report.</i></p>\"\n",
    "      ],\n",
    "      \"text/plain\": [\n",
    "       \"<IPython.core.display.HTML object>\"\n",
    "      ]\n",
    "     },\n",
    "     \"metadata\": {},\n",
